      "source": [
        "**Which of the three should you use?**\n",
        "\n",
        "All three produce the same fitted model. They differ in *who* builds the dummy variables and *when*.\n",
        "\n",
        "| | What it does | Use it when |\n",
        "|--|---|---|\n",
        "| **1. `C()` in the formula** | statsmodels creates the dummies while fitting. Nothing is added to your DataFrame. | Default for this course. You want a fitted model and a coefficient table to interpret. |\n",
        "| **2. `pd.get_dummies`** | You create the dummy columns yourself, up front, as real columns in the DataFrame. | You need a plain numeric design matrix — most commonly for `sklearn`, which cannot read formulas. |\n",
        "| **3. `pd.Categorical`** | Neither. It sets the *type* and the *level order* of the column; dummies are still made later by 1 or 2. | You care which level is the reference, or the categories have a natural order (`Bad` < `Medium` < `Good`). |\n",
        "\n",
        "Two consequences worth remembering:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Approaches 1 and 2 both drop one level to avoid perfect collinearity. `C()` does it automatically, `get_dummies` only if you pass `drop_first=True`. If you forget that argument, the design matrix is singular.\n",
        "- Approach 3 is not an alternative to the other two, it is a step *before* them. Setting the category order is how you control which level becomes the reference in Exercise 2.1."
      ]
    },
