        "---\n",
        "## Appendix\n",
        "\n",
        "### `statsmodels.api` vs `statsmodels.formula.api`\n",
        "\n",
        "The setup cell imports both. They are two entry points to the same models.\n",
        "\n",
        "**`smf`** (`statsmodels.formula.api`) takes a **formula string and a DataFrame**. Function names are lowercase. It adds the intercept for you and reads `C()` factors, `:` interactions and `I()` transformations straight out of the formula:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "smf.ols('medv ~ lstat + C(chas)', data=Boston).fit()\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "**`sm`** (`statsmodels.api`) takes **arrays**: a response vector and a design matrix you build yourself. Class names are uppercase, and you must add the intercept column explicitly — omit it and the regression is forced through the origin:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "X = sm.add_constant(Boston[['lstat']])\n",
        "sm.OLS(Boston['medv'], X).fit()\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Both return the same fitted-results object, so `.summary()`, `.params`, `.resid`, `.fittedvalues` and `.predict()` behave identically either way. This lab uses `smf` throughout because the formula syntax maps directly onto the notation used in the lecture.\n",
        "\n",
        "`sm` is still worth importing, because it carries utilities that `smf` does not expose: `sm.add_constant`, `sm.stats.anova_lm` for partial $F$-tests, and `sm.qqplot` and `sm.graphics.influence_plot` for residual diagnostics. You will start using these in Lab 3.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why Demo 2 mentions `sklearn`\n",
        "\n",
        "Approach 2 above builds dummy columns with `pd.get_dummies` and calls it \"useful for sklearn workflows\". Here is the reason, in brief.\n",
        "\n",
        "`scikit-learn` is the other main modelling library in Python. It does not accept formulas. Every model takes a numeric matrix `X` and a vector `y`, so categorical columns must already be dummy-coded using `pd.get_dummies`.\n",
        "\n",
        "The deeper difference is what each library is *for*:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **statsmodels** is built for **inference** — coefficients, standard errors, $p$-values, confidence intervals, diagnostics. Its `.summary()` is a statistics table.\n",
        "- **sklearn** is built for **prediction** — fitting, cross-validating and comparing many models on held-out data. It has no `.summary()`, and does not report many of the standard inferential output.\n",
        "\n",
        "This course uses `statsmodels` throughout the regression block because the questions are interpretive: what does this coefficient mean, and how sure are we? We take up `sklearn` properly in **Lesson 7**, where the question changes to how well a model predicts data it has not seen, and the train/test split and cross-validation machinery becomes the point.\n"
